\par\par \b \uc2 EXCLUSIVE-U.N. report accuses Yemen government of money-laundering, Houthis of taking state revenue\b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2021\par 20:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Independent U.N. sanctions monitors accused Yemen's government, in a report seen by Reuters on Tuesday, of money-laundering and corruption "that adversely affected access to adequate food supplies" and said the Houthi group collected at least $1.8 billion in state revenue in 2019 to help fund its war effort.\par \par \uc2 The annual report to the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  on the implementation of international sanctions on Yemen coincides with \uc2 U.N\uc2 . officials saying that the country is on the verge of a large-scale famine with millions of civilians at risk.\par \par \uc2 The monitors said Saudi Arabia deposited $2 billion with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Yemen\uc2  in January 2018 under a development and reconstruction program. The money was intended to fund credit to buy commodities - such as rice, sugar, milk and flour - to strengthen food security and stabilize domestic prices.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . investigation found that \uc2 Yemen's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules, manipulated the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and "laundered a substantial part of the Saudi deposit in a sophisticated money-laundering scheme" that saw traders receive a $423 million windfall.\par \par \uc2 "The $423 million is public money, which has been illegally transferred to private corporations. Documents provided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Yemen\uc2  fail to explain why they adopted such a destructive strategy," according to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . report.\par \par \uc2 The monitors said they view it as "an act of money-laundering and corruption perpetrated by government institutions, in this case the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Yemen\uc2  and the Government of Yemen, in collusion with well-placed businesses and political personalities, to the benefit of a select group of privileged traders and businessmen."\par \par \uc2 Yemen's government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment on the accusations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . report said that in areas controlled by the Houthis the group was collecting taxes and other state revenue needed to pay government salaries and provide basic services to citizens. It estimates the Houthis diverted at least $1.8 billion in 2019, "a large portion" of which was used to fund their war effort.\par \par \uc2 The Houthis did not immediately respond to a request for comment on the accusations in the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . report.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2 -led military coalition \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Yemen in 2015, backing government forces fighting the Houthis in a war widely seen as a proxy conflict between U.S. ally Saudi Arabia and Iran. \uc2 U.N\uc2 . officials are trying to revive peace talks to end the war as Yemen's suffering is also worsened by an economic collapse and the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . monitors reported "there is a growing body of evidence that shows that individuals or entities within the Islamic Republic of Iran are engaged in sending weapons and weapons components to the Houthis" in violation of a \uc2 U.N\uc2 . arms embargo. Iran denies such support for the Houthis. (Reporting by Michelle Nichols in New York; Additional reporting by Aziz El Yaakoubi in Dubai, Mohammed Ghobari in Aden and Nazanine Moshiri in Nairobi; editing by Grant McCool ) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-1-26T20:14:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210126eh1q03lv1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
