\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 "Oil crash is more concerned than coronavirus": Munir Jalil\b0\par\par\uc2 1,145 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 There is not a single place in the world where there is no talk of coronavirus, and the panic that has been unleashed by its contagion transcends every corner of the planet. For many, this pandemic, as the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  called it at the beginning of the week, is the hardest it has felt on a global scale and that in economic terms has impacted all productive sectors.However, amid the jitters of the outbreak, another phenomenon also shook the world, but this time international markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , entrepreneurs and thousands of ordinary citizens felt it very strongly.This is the sharp drop in oil prices, which dragged them by nearly 50% in less than three months. Of about $69 worth a barrel of crude oil on January 3, 2020, it plummeted to $33 last Friday.\par \par \uc2  Far from being a virus the protagonist of that decline, the collapse was caused by a disagreement between two major producing countries: Russia and Saudi Arabia.Russia did not want to abide by the pact to reduce production levels by supply, which generated a 'war' of prices with the Arabs. The idea was to lower production because China, which is the largest crude oil plaintiff, contracted purchases because of the impact of coronavirus.One of the main consequences of the oil price crash is the rise of the dollar to historic levels. Friday was $4,034, a figure no analyst had on his radar.\par \par \uc2 Read also: Businesses that are on the rise for coronavirus in ColombiaFor Colombia, this picture is very delicate because oil is our main export product, almost for every dollar we export, US$0.50 is oil sales.According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for every dollar that lowers the price of oil on international markets, the country's tax revenues fall by $400 billion.El Pa\u237\'00\'EDs spoke with cale\u241\'00\'F1o Munir Jalil Barney, chief economist for the Andean region of the Brazilian bank BTG Pactual, on this issue, and said that the oil issue is more sensitive than the crownvirus itself.How have you seen the dollar's behavior?Until last week he would have said that it would not reach the $4000, that the market reaction did not give for such volatility, however this week taught us a little humility, because markets when they are volatile take it seriously.We are seeing the consequences of the virus's expansion, especially in the developed world and the big question is whether it will generate a recession globally. That's the big fear in the markets today.I think there will be more weakness of our coins.For our country, what can you expect?In our case I can say that Colombia has suffered two crashes. One is the coronavirus and the other is the fall in the price of oil.This combination of factors has made us see a very complex situation, of a lot of exchange rate volatility and today higher levels cannot be ruled out until there is a bit of calm in terms of coronavirus, and the shock so sharp fall of the russia, which is the responsibility of Russia and Saudi Arabia.Which one affects the country the most?I say we're affected the second crash the most. The oil drop is most impactful, the weakness we see in the price of oil is neither positive nor constructive, and that has brought the dollar to very high levels. A 50% drop in the price of oil makes it difficult for dollars to enter the country.\par \par \uc2 See also: CoffeeColombian, benefited amid the stormy decline in world markets\par \par \uc2 "Exchange rate volatility will continue until an agreement is reached between the Opep countries, but collaboration is neededthird party."Munir Jalil Barney,Chief economist of BTG Pactual for the Andean regionIs that to say that the picture is delicate in Colombia?Of the two shocks, one could say that with coronavirus what has been observed in other countries is that in about ten weeks, about three months, you can stop talking about the outbreak.You can see that in China the number of contagions is depleting and it seems that they are gradually having it under control. Then I would say that, in the worst case, one will be able to continue talking about coronavirus until the middle of the year.But the shock of the price of oil is more delicate and a little more uncertain, because a large effort is needed. It is no longer in the hands of Russia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but by a third party that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that we can see a settlement and make peace with these two countries.We need to see an agreement that leads to a reduction in oil production, but for that we are further away and it is a situation of high volatility that creates uncertainty.Do you think in June the world will stop talking about the virus?One would think that there will be no talk beyond the middle of the year, but the price of oil could, unfortunately, continue to speak beyond June. That impact is what affects us the most.Can that speed up the recession in the world?Look, coronavirus is leading to many companies being affected, such as those in the tourism and airline industries. These companies have bank financing and if they stop paying the bank, they will be bankrupt.That's where the common thread lies in saying that a global recession can break out along the way of the financial system.Low oil prices also make some producers in the United States go bankrupt, and if that happens, they won't be able to pay their debts to the financial system either. There are the risks. The economic impact ends up translating into a financial crisis, so those who talk about a possible recession refer to an impact via financial channel.If there is no agreement between the tankers by June, what can happen in the economy?Well, look, you have to be looking at the picture and keeping an eye on the prices. If companies are going to break up, that impact will be felt by the bank and that's what has the markets as they are, it's all an uncertainty that can translate into a recession. We have to understand that in the medium and long term I don't think we're going to be talking about coronavirus. Not even in six months.For us, the most sensitive issue is oil, because there is no solution without the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of a third party, it is necessary for a third nation to help and act. However, I am optimistic that once you turn the page of these episodes, the global economy will begin a process of recovery.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200315eg3f001f0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
