\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-The mysterious (and continuing) fall in Saudi foreign reserves\b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2017\par 12:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2017 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Net foreign assets falling despite narrower budget deficit\par \par \uc2 * Rate of decrease almost the same as in 2015 and 2016\par \par \uc2 * Main reason may be transfers to sovereign wealth fund\par \par \uc2 * Fresh oil price slump likely to keep assets falling\par \par \uc2 * Assets still more than ample to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Andrew Torchia\par \par \uc2 RIYADH, June 27 (Reuters) - Net foreign assets at \uc2 Saudi Arabia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a measure of its ability to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , look set to fall sharply this year as oil prices slump and Riyadh expands its sovereign wealth fund to invest abroad.\par \par \uc2 They shrank from a record high of $737 billion in August 2014 to $529 billion at the end of 2016 as the government liquidated some assets to cover the huge budget deficit caused by the fall in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 This year, an austerity drive and a partial rebound in oil prices have helped Riyadh make progress in cutting the deficit -- which narrowed 71 percent from a year ago to 26 billion riyals ($6.9 billion) in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 But net foreign assets have continued to shrink at about the same rate, by $36 billion in the first four months of 2017 -- a mystery to economists and diplomats monitoring Saudi Arabia, and a potential blow to markets' confidence in Riyadh.\par \par \uc2 "This suggests that there remains a significant deficit in the balance of payments of Saudi Arabia, which is not due to declining oil export revenues," said Khatija Haque, head of regional research at \uc2 Emirates NBD\uc2 , Dubai's biggest bank.\par \par \uc2 Saudi officials have not commented in detail on the reasons for the reserves drop, though some have suggested it is due to private sector activity, not government spending.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have speculated the fall is due to spending on \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 This is unlikely; a top Saudi official indicated in late 2015 that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- largely a limited air campaign, not a major ground war -- was costing about $7 billion annually, in line with estimates by foreign military experts.\par \par \uc2 Others speculate capital flight from Saudi Arabia may be sapping the reserves. But data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the \uc2 Saudi Arabian \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (SAMA) -- on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions by commercial banks does not support this theory either.\par \par \uc2 "Capital flight has diminished as an issue. Outflows in 2016 were pretty small beer compared with 2015, when there were significant outflows," said an economist at a Saudi bank.\par \par \uc2 An international banker in touch with Saudi authorities said much of the decline in foreign assets appeared due to the transfer of money to state funds investing abroad -- particularly the main sovereign wealth fund, the \uc2 Public Investment Fund\uc2  (PIF).\par \par \uc2 Riyadh plans to invest big amounts overseas to win access to technology and boost returns on its capital. The PIF has said it will invest up to $45 billion over five years in a technology fund created by Japan's Softbank, and $20 billion in an infrastructure fund planned by U.S. firm Blackstone.\par \par \uc2 Transfers to the PIF would not represent any reduction in the government's total wealth, but they would mean a cut in the liquid assets which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has available to defend the riyal if needed. The PIF declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 OIL\par \par \uc2 A fresh slump in oil prices also looks likely to pressure foreign assets. Brent oil averaged $54.57 in the first quarter of this year; it has since plunged to around $46, just $1 above its average price last year.\par \par \uc2 This, combined with a minor relaxation of austerity in recent weeks to head off a recession, may mean Riyadh's budget deficit for all of 2017 comes close to its original projection of 198 billion riyals ($52.79 billion), or possibly a little higher.\par \par \uc2 That would be an improvement from last year's 297 billion riyals, but combined with transfers to the PIF, it would force further liquidation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign assets.\par \par \uc2 "Going forward, the decline is likely to continue given the projected budget deficit for the whole year, which would probably require withdrawals from foreign reserves to finance it," said Said al-Sheikh, group chief economist at \uc2 National Commercial Bank\uc2 , the largest Saudi bank.\par \par \uc2 Riyadh's heavy foreign borrowing -- it issued $9 billion of sukuk abroad in April -- gives it financial flexibility but does not increase its net foreign assets, since the debt is recorded as a liability, Sheikh said.\par \par \uc2 The government has said it plans to resume domestic bond issues later this year after a gap of more than half a year. A senior finance ministry official estimated last month that local bonds would cover 25 to 35 percent of the 2017 deficit; this would leave a sizeable amount to be covered with foreign assets.\par \par \uc2 At $493 billion as of the end of April, SAMA's net foreign assets remain enough to defend the riyal for years -- they would pay for four years of imports.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, a rise in the cost of insuring Saudi debt against default this month, to its highest level since early February, suggests concern may be increasing. ($1 = 3.7507 riyals)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Marwa Rashad Editing by Jeremy Gaunt)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2017-6-30T10:29:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020170627ed6r00dt9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
