\par\par \b \uc2 Saudis to set strategy for era of cheap oil as red ink flows\b0\par\par\uc2 891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2015\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * 2016 budget expected to be released around Dec. 21\par \par \uc2 * Multi-year economic plan to follow\par \par \uc2 * King's son Mohammed bin Salman puts mark on policy\par \par \uc2 * Spending cuts may roughly halve 2016 deficit\par \par \uc2 * Taxes, fuel subsidy cuts expected in long term\par \par \uc2 By Andrew Torchia and Marwa Rashad\par \par \uc2 DUBAI/RIYADH, Dec 13 (Reuters) - Saudi Arabia's government is expected to announce spending cuts and a drive to raise revenue from new sources as it lays out a strategy to cope with an era of cheap oil, people familiar with Saudi policy-making said.\par \par \uc2 Markets in the world's top oil exporter are jittery because low crude prices have pushed state finances deep into deficit and so far, the government has not revealed a detailed, comprehensive plan to stem the flow of red ink.\par \par \uc2 But in coming weeks, authorities will make their intentions clearer. The state budget for 2016 is expected to be released on or around Dec. 21, official sources said.\par \par \uc2 In the following weeks, probably in January, the government is to reveal a multi-year economic plan that may include longer-term reforms such as cuts to energy subsidies and new taxes.\par \par \uc2 The budget will be the first drafted by the administration of King Salman, who took the throne in January, and the first carrying the imprint of his son Mohammed bin Salman, who chairs a powerful new Council of Economic and Development Affairs that now dominates the economic policy apparatus.\par \par \uc2 So far Mohammed bin Salman, also defence minister, has focused much of his energy on launching \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Yemen. He is now expected to apply some of that willingness to take radical action to the economy.\par \par \uc2 "A strategic review of economic policy is underway in the government and officials are putting together a new framework for managing the economy," said Khalid Alsweilem, a former senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now fellow at the Harvard Kennedy School's Belfer Center in Boston.\par \par \uc2 He said much of the work was occurring at the Ministry of Economy and Planning, where Adel al-Fakieh became minister in April. As labour minister in 2010-2015, Fakieh developed a reputation for implementing complex reforms.\par \par \uc2 Under Fakieh, the economy ministry has gained influence while the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become less central, Alsweilem said. "There's a 180 degree change in the way policy-making is being done." The two ministries did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 <-----------------------------------------------------------\par \par \uc2 Saudi spending, revenue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/gap75t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/gap75t}}}\par \par \uc2 ----------------------------------------------------------->\par \par \uc2 CUTS\par \par \uc2 The government's deficit this year is expected to come in at about 400-500 billion riyals ($107-133 billion), around 20 percent of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 To ease market jitters, the government will need to cut next year's deficit sharply. Prominent Saudi economists contacted by Reuters expect the 2016 budget to plan spending of about 800 billion riyals, roughly 20 percent lower than their estimate of this year's actual spending.\par \par \uc2 The government is likely to curb public sector wage rises and bonuses, but that is politically sensitive, so most spending cuts would occur in public investment.\par \par \uc2 "The state has delivered a lot of infrastructure projects, so spending should normally decline gradually over the next few years," said Mazen al-Sudairi, research chief at al-Istithmar Capital.\par \par \uc2 In the past Riyadh has often overspent its annual budget plan, but one change introduced by Mohammed bin Salman is expected to be stricter adherence to the plan. He told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  last month that one of his key challenges was "the way we prepare and spend our budgets".\par \par \uc2 The result, if Brent oil stays around $40 a barrel next year, could be a budget deficit of around 200 billion riyals - still large, but enough of a reduction to let Riyadh slow the liquidation of its foreign assets.\par \par \uc2 REFORMS\par \par \uc2 If oil stays cheap for years, deeper reforms will be needed to stabilise state finances; the multi-year economic plan may prepare the ground for this.\par \par \uc2 Officials have said they are looking at raising domestic energy prices, potentially saving some of the over $100 billion spent annually to keep prices low.\par \par \uc2 Subsidy cuts would start with rises in the cost of natural gas feedstock and power for industry; politically difficult hikes in domestic petrol prices would come later and be spread over years, said a source familiar with official thinking.\par \par \uc2 The government is also considering privatisations and new taxes. The cabinet has approved a tax on undeveloped urban land that could be introduced as soon as the end of next year.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia and five other Gulf Arab states have stepped up talks on imposing a value-added tax across the region; a United Arab Emirates official said governments were aiming to introduce it in three years.\par \par \uc2 Alsweilem said that in the long term, it would not be enough for Riyadh to increase domestic revenue sources - authorities would need to develop non-oil sources of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make up for oil revenues lost because of low prices.\par \par \uc2 Increasing non-oil exports would happen only slowly, so Riyadh should also consider introducing new policies to preserve its foreign assets and create a sovereign wealth fund to increase returns on them, he said. (Editing by Raissa Kasolowsky) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-13T12:09:42.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151213ebcd00acb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
