\par\par \b \uc2 Saudi Arabia Hits Global Bond Market to Plug Deficit; Where Are the Budget Cuts? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Johanna Bennett   \par \uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2015\par 19:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia's decision to tap the global bond markets to cork a massive budget deficit is a history-making event. It is also a sign of the damage done to the Middle Eastern kingdom's public finances by the global rout in crude oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Details are hazy, but according to The \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 , work is finalizing on an international bond issuance program expected to start early next year, and several banks have already submitted proposals.\par \par \uc2 The first sale, according to reports, is expected in the $1 billion to $2 billion range, with three to four sales taking place annually.\par \par \uc2 According to The FT, Saudi officials say the kingdom's debt levels could spike to as much as 50% of GDP within the next half decade, compared to the 6.7% forecast for this year and 17.3% in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The move comes as no surprise, says Jason Tuvey, an economist with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . With oil sitting stubbornly below $50 a barrel and high budget spending on the back of bonuses paid to public workers and military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Yemen, Tuvey says \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces a 2015 budget deficit equal to 20% of GDP, narrowing to 15% next year.\par \par \uc2 But what about budget cuts? As Tuvey writes:\par \par \uc2 As we've argued for a while, Saudi Arabia's large fiscal buffer means that it can afford to prolong the adjustment to cheap oil. (See our Update, "How large is Saudi Arabia's fiscal buffer?", 7th September.) However, the authorities have yet to outline how they plan to push ahead with fiscal consolidation. This was one of the key reasons why S&P decided to downgrade Saudi Arabia's credit rating last month and the \uc2 IMF\uc2  has recently urged the authorities to be more transparent with their fiscal plans.\par \par \uc2 Next month's budget statement for 2016 should provide some clarity. If history is any guide, we suspect that capital spending (e.g. infrastructure projects) will bear the brunt of the adjustment. Indeed, recent reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has ordered government entities to halt new projects and postpone purchases of new cars and furniture suggest that this is the path that will be followed.\par \par \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  knocked the Saudi government's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit rating down to A-plus from double A last month. It sits four notches below the top triple-A rating, but well above the credit ratings for many developing nations.\par \par \uc2 It was a badly timed move. In June, Saudi Arabia lightened foreign ownership restrictions on its domestic stock market.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices, meanwhile, could remain depressed for some time. The \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2  warned today that \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's refusal to cut production could depress crude prices until the end of the decade.\par \par \uc2 The new \uc2 iShares MSCI Saudi Arabia Capped ETF\uc2  ( KSA) rose 1.3% Tuesday, while funds covering the broader region lost ground. The \uc2 WisdomTree Middle East Dividend Fund\uc2  ( GULF) fell 0.4% and The Market Vectors Gulf States Index ETF ( MES) fell 1.55%.\par \par \uc2 See The \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  story, " Saudi Arabia to Tap Global Bond Markets as Oil Fall Hits Finances," and our blog posts " Blame Oil: Saudi Arabia Gov't Dollar Debt Downgraded," " New Saudi ETF: Light On Energy?" and " Saudi Arabia In Bear Market As Oil, Debt Outlook Sink."\par \par \uc2 Also see Dimitra DeFotis's January column, " New World for Saudi Stocks." (Subscription required.). \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 10, 2015 13:57 ET (18:57 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151110ebba003g0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
