\par\par \b \uc2 Investors Bet That  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Pegs Will Come Under Strain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anjani Trivedi in Hong Kong and Chiara Albanese in London   \par \uc2 908 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2015\par 20:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 With global markets thrown into a twist, some investors are betting that long-standing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs might come under strain.\par \par \uc2 Traders and investors said a buildup in bets against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs in Hong Kong and Saudi Arabia accelerated after \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month. The wagers picked up further after Kazakhstan and Vietnam moved to free their exchange rates, too.\par \par \uc2 "[Investors] are looking at who's next," said Cynthia Wong, head of \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale SA\uc2 's emerging-market Asia fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in Hong Kong and Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Policy makers in places including \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in narrow trading bands. Wild swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market can make that task more difficult.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's 32-year-old peg to the U.S. dollar, backed by its de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, has weathered bouts of volatility. The \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  closely monitors the peg against the U.S. dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tests the limits of HK$7.75 or HK$7.85 ($1 or $1.01) against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Saudi \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has effectively fixed the riyal's exchange rate with the U.S. dollar since 1986. Some analysts said the peg could be vulnerable due to the rapid fall in the price of oil.\par \par \uc2 "Saudi is a big oil exporter, and oil-exporting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been hit hard this year," said Chris Turner, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING in London.\par \par \uc2 A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official responded to the speculation on Tuesday, telling Saudi Arabia's Al Arabiya news channel that the kingdom is committed to pegging the riyal to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Saudi and Hong Kong central banks wouldn't lightly abandon the pegs, which provide a degree of stability to their economies, most analysts said. Still, there are signs that some traders believe the pegs could be strained in the current market turmoil, which has led to erratic moves in some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There has been a surge in bets in the options markets that the Hong Kong dollar and Saudi Arabian riyal could see increased volatility over the next few months. Futures markets indicate that investors expect both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken despite their pegs over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 In the Hong Kong dollar options market, in which investors can make bets to protect against a change in Hong Kong's peg against the U.S. dollar over the next month, the cost of such positions has surged to its highest level in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 "When everyone is talking about pegs, the Hong Kong dollar will come into consideration," said \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\uc2 's Ms. Wong.\par \par \uc2 A representative from the \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said "the exchange rate and interest rates have remained steady" and "there is no sign of significant net inflows or net outflows of funds." The representative said the Hong Kong government "is fully committed" to maintaining its peg.\par \par \uc2 Recent concerns about China's economy have put the Hong Kong dollar under the spotlight, said \uc2 Ian Gunner\uc2 , a fund manager at \uc2 Altana\uc2  Wealth in London.\par \par \uc2 "The people making the markets feel there is an increased risk" of the peg breaking, he said. "If you want to bet on the peg being removed, it is going to cost you a lot more than before."\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Gunner noted that Hong Kong is "wedded to the idea of exchange-rate stability" and said the payoff for betting against the peg is less favorable than it was a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 BNP Paribas SA\uc2  economist Mole Hau said the rise in bets on increased swings in Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes as investors and companies convert funds from Chinese yuan into Hong Kong dollars. "Safe-haven demand for the Hong Kong dollar has been rising," he said.\par \par \uc2 Strains in global markets have forced central banks in several countries to abandon long-held \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. Late last year, \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that it would let the ruble float freely as the country's economy came under stress. Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned a cap on the value of the franc earlier this year, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaring against the euro.\par \par \uc2 "The market appears to have turned more nervous about regime shifts for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs," based on moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards and options markets, strategists from \uc2 Barclays PLC\uc2  wrote in a note Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Kazakhstan's government decided to remove the dollar peg for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the tenge, as prices for copper and oil fell. The decision led to a depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of more than 20% in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Some investors said Kazakhstan's move, which followed soon after the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, helped prompt traders to look for other pegs that could break.\par \par \uc2 "Following Kazakhstan's abandonment of its peg to the dollar, we have seen increased trading activity on our option desk with hedge funds speculating in devaluations in Saudi Arabia and Hong Kong," said Per Hammarlund, economist at Swedish bank SEB.\par \par \uc2 Laurence Fletcher and Carolyn Cui contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anjani Trivedi at anjani.trivedi@wsj.com and Chiara Albanese at chiara.albanese@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 25, 2015 14:40 ET (18:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150825eb8p002un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
