\par\par \b \uc2 Gulf Monetary Council to Tackle Single  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Peg, Launch\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dania Saadi and Summer Said   \par \uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2009\par 17:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of ZAWYA DOW JONES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KUWAIT CITY (Zawya Dow Jones)--A Gulf monetary council will be tasked with deciding on the peg of a proposed single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  among Saudi Arabia, Bahrain, Qatar and Kuwait and timeline to launch the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Kuwait's foreign minister said on Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "Whether this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be pegged to the dollar or a basket or something else is a technical matter that will be decided by the monetary council," Sheikh Mohammed Al Sabah said at a press conference at the end of a meeting of the Gulf Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Council in Kuwait. "The council will be tasked with setting a number of standards, such as the percentage of deficit to gross domestic product."\par \par \uc2 The launch of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be preceded by coordination of monetary and fiscal policies and the creation of a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kuwait's foreign minister said.\par \par \uc2 Sabah was speaking after Gulf leaders ended on Tuesday a two-day meeting, which tackled a host of issues such as the monetary union, a cross-border power grid and security issues such as Saudi Arabia's ongoing problems with Yemeni rebels along the border.\par \par \uc2 The monetary council will also be tasked with deciding on a timeframe to launch the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the final communique of the summit. The Gulf monetary union agreement, which was ratified by Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, Qatar and Bahrain, has taken effect, a move that will pave the way to launch the much-delayed single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The United Arab Emirates, the second-largest Arab economy, pulled out from the project in May, citing dissatisfaction over the choice to base the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Saudi Arabia. The U.A.E. is the second Gulf state to abandon the monetary union plan after Oman's exit three years ago. Kuwaiti officials have said they will work toward convincing the United Arab Emirates and Oman to rejoin the project.\par \par \uc2 Kuwait will take over in 2010 the rotating presidency of the Gulf Cooperation Council, the loose-knit economic and political organization that groups Saudi Arabia, Oman, Bahrain, Kuwait, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates.\par \par \uc2 The monetary union project, which has been in the works since 2001, has been dogged by delays and debates over technical issues such as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. Kuwait complicated plans for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it broke ranks in 2007 and de-pegged its dinar from the U.S. dollar to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight soaring inflation. All other Gulf countries link their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, forcing them to mimic the monetary policy of the U.S. Federal Reserve despite high inflation, higher growth projections and differing credit conditions.\par \par \uc2 Customs Union Gulf states have agreed to complete all the steps required to set up a customs union for the region, council Secretary General Abdulrahman Al Attiyah said at the press conference. The customs union, which has been stuck in a transitional phase since 2003, would collect a 5% tariff on imports at the first port of entry in countries belonging to the GCC. It would also establish a common customs law.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Gulf leaders agreed to set up a rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  force that will help states to deal with any aggression against their territories, such as the Yemeni rebel incursion into \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Attiyah said." It was decided to create a rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  force...that will ward off any aggression against any member state," Attiyah said.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Saudi Arabia launched an offensive against Yemeni Houthi rebels along the two countries' border after unknown gunmen crossed the border and killed a Saudi border guard. The kingdom said it had set up a 10-kilometer buffer, or "kill," zone inside Yemen, adding it will shell the area to keep rebels from re-entering the kingdom. The Saudi navy also set up a blockade off Yemen's northern Red Sea coastline, in what the kingdom says is an effort to keep fresh supplies from reaching the rebels, who have engaged in a five-year insurgency in the north of the country to win autonomy.\par \par \uc2 Gulf leaders also approved Bahrain's candidate to take over the secretary general post from Attiyah in April 2011. Mohammed Al Mutawa, cultural affairs advisor to the Bahraini prime minister, will start a three-year term on April 1, 2011, after Attiyah's term ends, the final communique said.\par \par \uc2 -By Dania Saadi and Summer Said, Dow Jones Newswires; +9714-446-1681; dania.saadi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2009 ABQ Zawya Ltd. and \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . [ 12-15-09 1153ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020091215e5cf000h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
