\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Markets-Bond issues testify to risk appetite; stx soar   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sujata Rao   \par \uc2 1,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2007\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Emerging equities posted a record high for the fourth straight session on Thursday as expectations grew of another U.S. interest rate cut while a successful Turkish Eurobond sale testified to abundant investor appetite for risk.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. housing and employment data later in the day are widely seen as making the case for more policy easing even though high-yield assets are basking in the afterglow of last week's 50 basis point rate cut by the Federal Reserve. The yen fell back, indicating rising interest in carry trades.   \par \par \uc2 Emerging equities on the  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  benchmark index rose 1.5 percent to a new high, just short of 1200 points <.MSCIEF>. The index has gained over 30 percent so far this year, compared with 12 percent gains for the MSCI World index <.MIWD00000PUS>.   \par \par \uc2 Bond markets were likewise buoyant, with yield spreads on  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's EMBI Plus index contracting 4 basis points to 194 bps over U.S. Treasuries. Turkey's portion of the EMBI Plus tightened 2 bps to 211 bps over U.S. Treasuries.   \par \par \uc2 "There is appetite for sovereigns," said Kaushik Rudra, emerging debt strategist at  \uc2 Lehman Brothers.\uc2  "People are differentiating between corporates and sovereigns and have more confidence in the sovereign outlook than the corporate outlook."   \par \par \uc2 This view was confirmed by news that the order book for Ghana's debut $750 million Eurobond had come in at almost $3 billion. The deal will price later in the day and yield guidance on the bond has tightened to 8.5 percent, a market source said.   \par \par \uc2 The Turkey deal came with a bit of a concession to the curve, analysts said, noting it was 5-7 bps wider to the curve and about 4 bps wider than the existing 2010 issue.   \par \par \uc2 Bond traders said the yield on the issue had initially snapped in 10 bps on Thursday before rising back.   \par \par \uc2 "Most of the activity this morning has been in the Turkey '18s but the rest of EM is mostly quiet and trading cautiously ahead of the U.S. data releases, and given the strength we witnessed yesterday," Commerzbank trader Paul Timmons said.   \par \par \uc2 STILL SOME STRESS   \par \par \uc2 Rudra said investors were still cautious, reflected in the fact that credits less dependant on financial markets are outperforming. There is also some rotation of cash into the more liquid assets from the less liquid, he said.   \par \par \uc2 "The tone of the market is better. More risk is being put on but these two trends are definitely there within that -- a bit of differentiation and seeking of liquidity."   \par \par \uc2 The stresses manifested themselves in Russian money market rates which had soared to two-year highs around 10 percent on Wednesday. They eased to 8 percent on Thursday and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected 48 billion roubles ($2 billion) after putting in about 64 billion roubles in total on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Kazakhstan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also delayed a rise in banks' reserve requirements to Jan. 15, 2008 from the previously planned Oct. 9. to smooth the liquidity squeeze on local markets.   \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , South Africa's rand rose to a new 2-month high versus the dollar <ZAR=>, helped by gold prices near a 28-year high and platinum near a 10-month peak. It broke past the key resistance level of 6.93 and is now testing 6.88 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The lira hit a new six-year high <TRY=>, the best performing emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year with gains of 16.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The Czech crown was flat as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady as expected.   \par \par \uc2 Some of the upward pressure on the Saudi riyal subsided as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated its commitment to its dollar peg. But the riyal remains at 3.74 per dollar, above the peg level of 3.75.   \par \par \uc2 "Overall, the market hasn't been shaken and remains positive that the riyal peg will go,"  \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said in a note.   \par \par \uc2 "We continue to believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2  could indeed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term should 1-year forwards move below 300 pips but over the medium term we continue to believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime change is increasingly inevitable." [20070927 104715 GMT]   \par \par \uc2 Emerging Markets Prices from Reuters Equities Latest Net Chg % Chg % Chg   \par \par \uc2 on year  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Emrg Mkt Indx  <.MSCIEF>  1198.05   +16.63   +1.41  +31.27\par  Czech Rep      <.PX>      1819.80    -5.50   -0.30  +14.53\par  Poland         <.WIG20>   3750.43   +54.61   +1.48  +14.15\par  Hungary        <.BUX>    28193.60  +345.41   +1.24  +13.48\par  Slovakia       <.SAX>      430.69    +0.00   +0.00   +3.63\par  Romania        <.BETI>    9492.14   -59.84   -0.63  +17.91\par  Russia         <.IRTS>    2057.18    +7.73   +0.38   +7.04\par  South Africa   <.JTOPI>  27350.52  +192.08   +0.71  +20.98\par  Turkey         <.XU100>  54445.79  +199.96   +0.37  +39.19\par  China          <.SSEC>    5409.40   +70.88   +1.33 +102.18\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2               Latest    Prev    Local    Local  \par                                 close   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par          