\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Gulf  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  - To peg or not to peg? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tara FitzGerald \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 1999\par 09:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DUBAI, Feb 18 (Reuters) - Is a dollar peg a guarantee of stability for Gulf Arab \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or an indefensible anachronism that is bound to fall away eventually no matter how much money is poured into it? \par \par \uc2  Analysts in the Gulf Arab states are divided on the issue, and although most authorities say they are determined to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  right down to the wire, not everyone is convinced that will be enough. \par \par \uc2  The oil-rich Gulf Arab states, envied until recently for their seemingly unending wealth, are now labouring under sagging world oil prices which have hit their economies hard and forced them to adopt austerity budgets. \par \par \uc2  Their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most of which are pegged to the U.S. dollar because it is the medium for pricing oil and so accounts for most of their exports, could be the next victims, some analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The Saudi riyal, which came under attack from speculators last year, is the first example many economists cite, adding that if the riyal falls all the other Gulf Arab \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will tumble like so many dominos. \par \par \uc2  The Saudi riyal usually trades in a narrow band around its peg of 3.7500 to the dollar. Last August it came under pressure from foreign-based speculators who were betting on a devaluation as world oil prices declined. \par \par \uc2  Government sources said the Saudi Arabian Monetary Agency (SAMA) or \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped some $750 million into the market to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We have a very different situation now to what has happened in past years - you have got a chronic oversupply situation in the oil market and a troubled world economy, so it's not too encouraging really," said one Bahrain-based banker who asked not to be named. \par \par \uc2  But the Saudi Minister of Finance and National Economy, Ibrahim Abdel-Aziz al-Assaf, categorically ruled out any devaluation of the riyal late last year, saying there was no economic reason to take such a step. \par \par \uc2  Will the government be able to put its money where its mouth is if the riyal is targeted again? \par \par \uc2  "Looking at all the fundamentals and everything that goes into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when the government here says they are not going to devalue I believe them," Kevin Taecker, chief economist at Saudi American Bank (SAMBA) , told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The Saudi government traditionally defends the riyal through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than by increasing interest rates. \b \highlight22\uc2 Saudi Arabia\b0 \highlight\uc2  last devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1986. \par \par \uc2  Diplomats in Riyadh said they estimated that the Saudi government would spend up to $20 billion in defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before eventually relenting. \par \par \uc2  They said devaluation could come within the next 18 months, while other bankers said it could occur as soon as the second half of this year. \par \par \uc2  But some economists felt the Gulf Arab states would not fall victim to the weaknesses exposed in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "You have economies which are much less dependent on bank credit. They are not as highly geared on credit and are not as open to foreign investors," one Saudi-based economist said. \par \par \uc2  "There is also strong emphasis in Saudi Arabia to achieve higher levels of private sector growth and what is important in that is Saudi private capital... it is widely felt there is a lot of Saudi capital offshore and to attract that capital back it is important to maintain confidence in the peg," he said. \par \par \uc2  Bankers in Bahrain, the Gulf's main financial and banking hub, agreed but said this was exactly the danger that threatened regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It is largely a question of reputation and any loss of confidence in the riyal could cause locals to pull their money out of all the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one said. \par \par \uc2  But delinking from the dollar as a result of a forced devaluation does not have to be a negative step. \par \par \uc2  It could be a structured part of the economic diversification most Gulf Arab states are currently attempting. \par \par \uc2  Gulf News daily recently reported the United Arab Emirates' minister of communications and former minister of state for finance, Ahmed Humaid al-Tayer, saying if "we manage to delink our oil products from the dollar, we would be able to create a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid possible future political dangers. \par \par \uc2  "We would then have the choice to export our products in dollar, yen or other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and...avoid cases where one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be frozen. We can then export to countries dealing in euro with euro and in dollar with dollar." ((Gulf newsroom, +971 4 607 1222, fax +971 4 626982, dubai.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2i048xi\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clv